When valid is zero, every thread initializes its slot to zero and reaches the barrier. Thread zero executes zero loop iterations and writes `0.0f` to the output itself; the host neither initializes the output nor skips the kernel. Only thread zero writes the output. Event synchronization checks completion before the host reads it, but successful execution alone is not a proof of correctness—the initialization, bounds, and barrier reasoning above provide that proof.

