In Alice’s connection:

```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT go_off_call('Alice');
COMMIT;
```

Concurrently, in Bob’s connection:

```sql
BEGIN TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE;
SELECT go_off_call('Bob');
COMMIT;
```

A serialization failure, SQLSTATE `40001`, can occur during a statement or at commit. Roll back if the transaction remains open, then retry the entire transaction: a fresh BEGIN, the count and conditional update through `go_off_call`, and COMMIT. Do not retry only the UPDATE or COMMIT, and do not reuse the earlier count. Repeated failures require repeated whole-transaction retries; report success only after COMMIT succeeds.

For correctness, assume at least one doctor is initially on call and every transaction that removes an on-call doctor follows this serializable protocol. In any serial execution, a transaction removes a doctor only when more than one is on call, so at least one remains. PostgreSQL serializable isolation ensures committed transactions have an equivalent serial execution; the two conflicting decisions cannot both commit. After retrying with a fresh snapshot following the other transaction’s commit, the remaining doctor sees a count of one and stays on call.

